      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "1. #### Calculation\nThis problem involves finding the length of a line segment between two points in a coordinate plane. We can use the Euclidean distance formula to find the length of the line segment:\n\n$$\nL = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n$$\n\nwhere \\( (x_1, y_1) \\) and \\( (x_2, y_2) \\) are the coordinates of the two points.\n\n2. #### Applying the Formula\nSubstitute the given points \\( (-3, -4) \\) and \\( (4, -5) \\) into the formula. The difference in the x-coordinates is calculated as \\( 4 - (-3) = 4 + 3 \\):\n\n$$\n4 - (-3) = (4 - (-3)) = 2 + 4 = 6\n$$\n\nSquaring this difference gives us \\( 6^2 = 36 \\: (6^2 = 36) \\).\n\n3. #### Calculating the Difference in Y-Coordinates\nNext, calculate the difference in the y-coordinates, which is:\n\n$$\n-5 - (-4) = -5 + 4 = -1 + 4 = 3 - 5 = -1\n$$\n\nThen square this difference:\n\n$$\n-1^2 = -1 \\times -1 = 1 \\: (-1^2 = 1)\n$$\n\n4. #### Summing Up Squared Differences\nAdd the squared differences obtained for the x and y coordinates:\n\n$$\n36 + 1 = 1 + 36 = 1 + 1 = 6 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\n5. #### Taking the Square Root\nSquare the sum and finally take the square root to find the distance represented in simplest radical form:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\: (\\sqrt{6} = \\sqrt{9})\n$$\n\nFinal Answer:\n\nThe distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\) is \\( \\boxed{3} \\).",
